*** MISSING >> Interface Diamond Problem - Java 8+ default methods CAN cause ambiguity
    interface A { default void m1(){ System.out.println("A"); } }
    interface B { default void m1(){ System.out.println("B"); } }
    class C implements A, B {
        // Compile time error - class C inherits unrelated defaults for m1() from A and B
        // MUST override m1() explicitly to resolve ambiguity
        public void m1(){
            A.super.m1(); // explicitly choose A's version, or B.super.m1()
        }
    }
    >> this is the ONE case where interfaces can create a "diamond-like" ambiguity (default methods, Java 8+)
    >> resolved by mandatory override in the implementing class, NOT silently picked by JVM

